# XBD 11.2 Parameters that Can be Set
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tests use a private pseudo-terminal (XSI) and set the input, output and local flags and the
# special characters through tcsetattr(); the manager side plays the user at the terminal.
# Control modes (c_cflag) and the baud rates of a pseudo-terminal are unspecified by the page
# (non-default values may be unsupported or have no effect), and break and parity errors cannot
# be injected through a pseudo-terminal, so those parts are accounted for in the .cov file. The
# header constants (distinct bits, values) are XBD.termios.h.001 to .013.

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.1
class      required
since      1988
says       struct termios has members c_iflag, c_oflag, c_cflag and c_lflag
           of type tcflag_t and an array c_cc of NCCS elements of type cc_t.
note       Checked at compile time with _Generic; no terminal is needed.
           Unsigned-ness of the types is XBD.termios.h.001.
test       t/001-struct-members.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode with ICRNL set and IGNCR clear, ab and a
           carriage return is read as ab and a newline; with ICRNL clear a
           carriage return is neither a delimiter nor translated (cd,
           carriage return does not complete a read; after a newline it is
           read as cd, carriage return, newline).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/002-icrnl-canon.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In non-canonical mode with IGNCR clear, a carriage return sent
           from the manager is read as a newline when ICRNL is set and as a
           carriage return when it is clear.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/003-icrnl-noncanon.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In non-canonical mode with ICRNL and IGNCR clear, a newline sent
           from the manager is read as a carriage return when INLCR is set
           and as a newline when it is clear.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/004-inlcr.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In non-canonical mode with IGNCR set, a carriage return between a
           and b sent from the manager is dropped (ab is read), both with
           ICRNL clear and with ICRNL set.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/005-igncr.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In non-canonical mode with CS8, the bytes c1 and e9 sent from the
           manager are read as 41 and 69 when ISTRIP is set and unchanged
           when it is clear.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
           UNRESOLVED when the terminal does not keep CS8.
test       t/006-istrip.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With IXON set (IXANY clear), the STOP byte in use sent from the
           manager holds back what the subsidiary side writes next (nothing
           arrives within 0.4 s) until the START byte is sent, and neither
           byte can be read on the subsidiary side.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
           Whether START and STOP can be changed is implementation-defined,
           so the bytes in use are read from the terminal.
test       t/007-ixon-flow.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With IXON clear, the STOP and START bytes in use sent from the
           manager are read on the subsidiary side as two data bytes, and
           what the subsidiary side writes after the STOP byte still reaches
           the manager.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/008-ixon-clear-read.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.2
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With IXON and IXANY set, after the STOP byte has suspended output
           (nothing arrives within 0.4 s), an ordinary byte (q) sent from
           the manager lets the held output through.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/009-ixany.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With OPOST clear, even with ONLCR, OCRNL, ONOCR and ONLRET set,
           the bytes a, newline, b, carriage return, c, tab, d, newline
           written on the subsidiary side arrive at the manager unchanged.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The output flags other than OPOST are XSI.
test       t/010-opost-clear.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       XSI: with OPOST and ONLCR set, a, carriage return, b, newline
           written on the subsidiary side arrive at the manager as a,
           carriage return, b, carriage return, newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/011-onlcr.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       XSI: with OPOST and OCRNL set (ONLCR clear), a, carriage return,
           b, newline written on the subsidiary side arrive at the manager
           as a, newline, b, newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/012-ocrnl.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.013
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       XSI: with OPOST and ONOCR set (ONLCR, OCRNL, ONLRET clear), after
           a first carriage return has put the column at 0, the output
           carriage return, a, b, c, carriage return, carriage return, d
           arrives as a, b, c, carriage return, d: the carriage return at
           column 0 is dropped, the one after text is sent, and the second
           of two in a row is at column 0 again.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL. The
           first carriage return is output only to make the column 0
           whatever it was; what arrives for it is not checked.
test       t/013-onocr.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.014
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       XSI: with OPOST and ONOCR set, the output a, b, newline, carriage
           return, c arrives as a, b, newline, c when ONLRET is set (the
           newline put the column at 0, so the carriage return is dropped)
           and in full when ONLRET is clear (the newline leaves the column
           unchanged).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL. Each
           run starts with a carriage return to bring the column to 0; what
           arrives for it is not checked.
test       t/014-onlret.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.015
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With OPOST and the tab delay type TAB3, a, tab, b written on
           the subsidiary side arrive as a, one or more spaces, b, with no
           tab.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The number of spaces is not stated.
           UNRESOLVED when the terminal does not keep TAB3 (delay fields are
           not guaranteed to be kept).
test       t/015-tab3.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.016
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.3
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With OPOST and OFILL set, text with no delay field set
           arrives unchanged; a newline with delay NL1 is followed by 2 fill
           characters, a carriage return with CR2 by 4, a backspace with BS1
           by 1 and a tab with TAB2 by 2; the fill character is NUL, or DEL
           when OFDEL is set.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The delay fields are not guaranteed to be
           kept by tcsetattr(): a delay type that is not kept is skipped,
           and the test is UNRESOLVED when none is kept. The fill follows
           the character that causes the delay.
test       t/016-ofill.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.017
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode with ECHO set (and the other echo flags and
           OPOST clear), abc and a newline sent from the manager come back
           to the manager as abc and a newline; with ECHO clear nothing
           comes back.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/017-echo-canon.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.018
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode with ECHO and ECHOE set, abc followed by ERASE
           sent from the manager comes back as abc and at least one more
           byte (the erasing).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The form of the erasing is not stated (a
           typical one is backspace, space, backspace), so only the presence
           of output beyond the echo of the typed text is checked. The case
           of nothing to erase is left to the implementation.
           See Q-187.
test       t/018-echoe.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.019
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode with ECHO and ECHOK set, abc followed by KILL
           sent from the manager comes back as abc and at least one more
           byte (the line erasing or a newline).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The form is one of two the page allows; only
           the presence of output beyond the echo of the typed text is
           checked.
           See Q-187.
test       t/019-echok.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.020
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       In canonical mode with ECHO clear and ECHONL set (OPOST clear),
           ab and a newline sent from the manager come back as just the
           newline; with ECHONL clear nothing comes back.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL.
test       t/020-echonl.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.021
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With ISIG clear, the INTR, QUIT and SUSP bytes sent from the
           manager cause no SIGINT, SIGQUIT or SIGTSTP in the session leader
           or in a child in the foreground group (0.4 s watched), and are
           read on the subsidiary side as three data bytes.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED.
test       t/021-isig-clear.c
timeout    40

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.022
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.5
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       With ISIG and NOFLSH set, the input abc, INTR, d sent from the
           manager makes SIGINT arrive at the session leader and the bytes
           a, b, c, d (INTR discarded) are all still readable.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. The controlling terminal is acquired by
           opening the subsidiary side in a session leader; how a terminal
           is acquired is implementation-defined, so a refusal is
           UNRESOLVED. Only the NOFLSH-set case is tested: the flush that
           happens without NOFLSH is not described in detail.
test       t/022-noflsh.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.023
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.1.9
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       When the terminal has a disabling value (fpathconf _PC_VDISABLE),
           setting ERASE, KILL, EOF and EOL to it is kept, and in canonical
           mode the former ERASE, KILL, EOF and EOL bytes inside a line are
           then read as plain data with the line ending only at the newline.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. NOTINUSE when the terminal has no disabling
           value.
test       t/023-vdisable.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.024
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.6
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       When the terminal has a disabling value v, with ICANON clear MIN
           set to v makes a read of one queued byte return it if v is 0 or 1
           and wait otherwise, and TIME set to v (MIN 0) makes an empty read
           return 0 after about v tenths of a second (at once for 0, still
           waiting after 3 s when v is above 35).
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. NOTINUSE when the terminal has no disabling
           value; a value of 20 to 35 is UNRESOLVED (too close to the
           alarm). Timing bounds are generous.
test       t/024-vmin-vdisable.c

assertion  XBD.11.2-terminal-parameters.025
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap11.html#11.2.6
class      conditional
when       BASE|XSI
since      1988
says       After tcsetattr() asks for other START and STOP bytes (1f and
           1d), the values tcgetattr() returns are the ones that work: with
           IXON set, sending the returned STOP byte holds back output and
           the returned START byte releases it.
note       XSI only for the pseudo-terminal set-up (posix_openpt() and its
           companions are XSI); the requirement itself is Base. A set-up
           that is not available is UNRESOLVED, or NOTINUSE when the
           facility is absent. A flag the interface lists must be kept by
           tcsetattr(); a terminal that drops it makes the test FAIL. An
           implementation that cannot change them may ignore the request but
           must return the bytes in use.
test       t/025-startstop-readback.c
